Eitri @VERSION@ -- Your Neovim, with Claude Code beside it.
Built from commit @COMMIT@ in the Ubuntu 24.04 release container. It runs on glibc 2.39, GTK 4.14
and WebKitGTK 2.40 (the webkitgtk-6.0 API) or newer.

0.2.1 brings companion mode (the agent panel beside your own nvim), turn review and workspace trust, and
Auto tabs now leave their decisions to Claude Code's own auto mode. Much of it is new enough that so far it has
been tried only in an isolated test session, not on real hardware: see "How much of this has been tried"
below.

## What's new in 0.2.1

**Beside your own editor**

- **Companion mode:** the agent panel as a window of its own, next to the nvim you already use (in a
  terminal, in tmux, or in a Neovim GUI). Add the bundled `eitri.nvim` plugin and run `:EitriPanel`, or run
  `eitri panel` from a shell (INSTALL.md, "Use it beside your own nvim").
- **`eitri split [DIR]`** opens upstream Neovide and the panel side by side, attached to each other; closing
  that Neovide closes the panel. It needs `neovide` on your `PATH`.
- **`Ctrl+h/j/k/l` moves focus between the panel and its editor** on sway, Hyprland and niri, and on GNOME
  with the bundled GNOME Shell extension, which you turn on yourself and which starts working at your next
  login (INSTALL.md, "GNOME: the extension"). On KDE, Eitri cannot move focus, so there the keys do nothing.

**The agent**

- **Turn review:** after a turn, `c` in BROWSE lists the files that changed on disk during it, with their
  hunks. `x` reverts a hunk or a file, `u` undoes that, `i` and `s` send comments and your reverts back to the
  agent, and `o` draws the hunks over the file in your nvim. The snapshots it needs are kept under
  `~/.local/state/eitri/`, never in your project; `review.enabled = false` turns it off.
- **Auto tabs use Claude Code's own auto mode.** Eitri still sees every tool call; one your saved rules do not
  allow is left to Claude Code's classifier, and what it refuses shows on the tool's row as
  `blocked by auto: <reason>`. Where Claude Code cannot run its auto mode, Eitri's own rules answer, as in 0.2.0.
- **Your own Claude Code configuration loads**, as it does in a terminal: your settings, hooks, plugins,
  skills, `CLAUDE.md` and permission rules. `eitri.config.set("agent.user_settings", false)` leaves it out.
- **A project's own Claude configuration loads only after you trust it.** The first session in a project
  with a `.claude/` directory, a `.mcp.json` or a `CLAUDE.md` lists the hooks, MCP servers and allow rules it
  found and asks `y`/`n`; a change to any of them asks again. `:trust` and `:untrust` on the panel's command line.
- **A turn that fails, stops at a limit, is interrupted or is lost says so** where it ended.
- **The last session's tabs come back** from the empty tab's dashboard (`s`), or at every launch with
  `agent.restore = "auto"`.

**Keys and the panel**

- **Your tmux prefix and prefix-table bindings are picked up** from tmux's own configuration files;
  `keymap.from_tmux = "off"` stops it.
- `j`/`k` move evenly over replies of very different heights, and CARET's `w`/`e`/`b` follow vim's word rules.
- Eitri has an app icon, and its desktop entry is now `cn.huntergrey.eitri.desktop`.

**Security fixes**

- A file whose name contained a line break could run a command in your nvim when it was opened from the
  panel. File names now reach nvim only as data.
- The panel runs only its own script (pinned by its hash); a link in a reply opens only `http`, `https` and
  `mailto`; permission cards show invisible and direction-changing characters as `⟨U+XXXX⟩`; and a saved rule
  can no longer start with a program that runs another one (`env`, `timeout`, `sh` and the like).
- `install.sh` refuses a machine without `ssh-keygen`, since it could not check the release signature there;
  `--insecure-skip-signature` goes on with the checksums alone.

## Upgrading from 0.2.0

- **The agent backend is rebuilt**, because it is pinned to a newer revision. `install.sh` does that as part
  of the update; after a `.deb`/`.rpm` upgrade, run `eitri setup` once more.
- **What a session loads changed.** 0.2.0 loaded a project's own `.claude/` settings, `.mcp.json` and
  `CLAUDE.md` without asking, and not your own Claude Code settings. 0.2.1 loads yours, and the project's only
  after the trust question, so each project with such files asks once.
- **Eitri has an icon now**, and its desktop entry is named after its application id,
  `cn.huntergrey.eitri.desktop` (it was `eitri.desktop`). Updating removes the old entry, so if you had pinned
  Eitri to your dash or dock, pin it again once. If you rerun a saved 0.2.0 `install.sh` to upgrade, that
  works too, but it cannot install the icon: run this release's `install.sh` once more to get it
  (INSTALL.md, "Updating").

**`v0.2.0` will stop building from git.** The public history of the Neovide fork was rewritten after 0.2.0
(only commit metadata changed; the files are the same), so the `v0.2.0` tag and every earlier public commit
point at fork commits that no branch holds any more. Build 0.2.1 or later from git. For 0.2.0 itself, its
own `install.sh` (the 0.2.0 release asset) with `--from-source` works only until GitHub stops serving those
old objects, and its `eitri-0.2.0-source.tar.gz` release asset, which carries the fork's files, rebuilds it
for good; this release's `install.sh` cannot build 0.2.0 from source. The 0.2.0 packages, tarball and
`install.sh` are not affected.

## How much of this has been tried

Everything new has automated tests, and the permission and trust paths were also run against the real
`claude` (2.1.284 and 2.1.288). Beyond that:

- **Seen working only in an isolated, headless test session** (scripted keys, not a person at a real
  desktop): companion mode on sway with a terminal nvim; `eitri split` with Neovide 0.16.2 on sway; the GNOME
  Shell extension in a headless GNOME Shell 50; turn review with its reverts and the nvim overlay; the trust
  question; the `blocked by auto` rows; the panel's new script policy.
- **Tests only, not yet seen on a screen:** restoring the last session's tabs, the tmux key import, the new
  `j`/`k` movement, the wording of a turn stopped by a usage limit, and the icon in a real dock.
- **Not tried at all yet:** Hyprland, niri, KDE, Neovim GUIs other than Neovide, and the extension in a real
  GNOME login.

None of it has been through real hardware and a person's hands yet; 0.3.0 waits for that and for a week of
daily use. What you may run into is listed in the known issues (`docs/known-issues.md` in the repository).

## Performance, against native Neovide

Typing is within 1 ms of native Neovide at the median and at the 99th percentile on the two machines
measured (an Intel laptop and an AMD desktop, GTK 4.22.5, 60 and 165 Hz; measured just before 0.2.0). Below
GTK 4.16, and while a reply is streaming, see the known issues. **One known gap:** at 165 Hz on the Intel
laptop, with other work keeping the machine 17-28 % busy, the editor drew 137-149 frames a second where
native Neovide drew 160 (86-93 %), and 111 (69 %) at 34 % busy, with one 83.6 ms frame in four runs. That was
measured before 0.2.0's typing-latency fix and has not been measured again since.

## Install

    curl --proto '=https' --proto-redir '=https' --tlsv1.2 -sSfL https://github.com/HunterGrey-cyber/eitri/releases/download/v@VERSION@/install.sh | sh -s -- --version @VERSION@

or install `eitri_@VERSION@_amd64.deb` / `eitri-@VERSION@-1.x86_64.rpm` and run `eitri setup`
once, or install from `eitri-@VERSION@-x86_64-linux.tar.gz` with
`sh install.sh --tarball eitri-@VERSION@-x86_64-linux.tar.gz --sums SHA256SUMS --sig SHA256SUMS.sig`
(or unpack it and run its own `bin/eitri setup` from inside the extracted directory).

Eitri needs `nvim` >= 0.10 somewhere it can find one -- either on `PATH`, or its own private copy
under `XDG_DATA_HOME/eitri/nvim` (never placed on `PATH`; none of the packages depend on a system
nvim). Ubuntu 24.04's own package is 0.9.5, below that floor. `install.sh` (the curl command above)
offers to fetch the official nvim release for you at install time if none new enough is found; run
`eitri setup --nvim-only` on its own at any later time -- after a `.deb`/`.rpm`/tarball install,
or again -- to get the same offer.

## The agent backend is built on your machine

No asset here contains Anthropic's Claude Agent SDK. `eitri setup` downloads it from npm and
builds the agent backend (the sidecar) on your own machine, from `@VERDANDI_ASSET@` (the public
Verdandi at @VERDANDI_REV@) and the official Node release pinned in `RELEASE`. The SDK is not open
source; its own licence applies to your copy of it, and `eitri setup` shows where to read it.

## Licences and source

This program statically links nvim-rs @NVIM_RS_VERSION@, which is licensed under the GNU LGPL-3.0;
its use is covered by that licence. The Corresponding Source for this release is
`@SOURCE_ASSET@` on this page -- **not** GitHub's automatic "Source code" archives, which lack the
Neovide fork submodule, the vendored Rust dependencies and the Skia archive the build linked. It
rebuilds offline, and `SOURCE` (installed at `share/licenses/eitri/SOURCE`) gives the exact recipe
for relinking against a modified nvim-rs. `THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES`, beside it, carries every other
notice.

